For centuries, Paris has attracted artists from around the world. As a result, Paris has acquired a reputation as the "City of Art". Italian artists were a profound influence on the development of art in Paris in the 16th and 17th centuries, particularly in sculpture and reliefs. Painting and sculpture became the pride of the French monarchy, and the French royal family commissioned many Parisian artists to adorn their palaces during the French Baroque and Classicism era. Sculptors such as Girardon, Coysevox, and Coustou acquired reputations as the finest artists in the royal court in 17th-century France. Pierre Mignard became the first painter to King Louis XIV during this period. In 1648, the Académie royale de peinture et de sculpture (Royal Academy of Painting and Sculpture) was established to accommodate the dramatic interest in art in the capital. ([Culture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Culture))

Article sections: [Etymology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Etymology) · [Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Administration) · [Cityscape](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Cityscape) · [Demographics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Demographics) · [Sports](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Sports) · [Infrastructure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Infrastructure) · [International relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#International_relations) · [In popular culture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#In_popular_culture)

